     and now also computes LifetimeIRMAA the same way (previously nominal,
     presumably a raw per-year sum — no longer present).
   - Deflator check: `deflator := ys.CumulativeInflation; if deflator <= 0 {
     deflator = 1}` is a byte-for-byte match of the guard in
     tax_optimizer.go:237-239's projectionToCandidate (verified by reading
     both). Same field (models.ProjectionYearSummary.CumulativeInflation),
     same zero-guard, same per-year-then-sum order of operations. No
     off-by-one: both loop over YearlySummaries and divide ys.Taxes by that
     year's own deflator before summing (not sum-then-divide, which would
     be wrong under a time-varying deflator).
   - Net vs gross inflation: confirmed CumulativeInflation (used here) is
     distinct from NetCumulativeInflation (engine/stepper.go:34-39, used
     only for spending-phase rebasing, per loop_helpers.go). The sweep uses
     the same field the Tax Optimizer's already-shipped LifetimeTaxReal
     uses, not the net variant — correct choice, not the wrong series.
   - EndingBalanceReal (handlers_sweep.go:131,
     proj.Months[...].PortfolioBalanceReal) traces to
     engine/month.go:161: `totalBalance / st.CumulativeInflation` — same
     deflator field, so all three money columns and the caption are
     internally consistent (verified, not assumed).
   - Caption now reads "Ending balance, lifetime tax, and lifetime IRMAA are
     all in today's dollars (inflation-deflated), summed across projection
     years" (conversion-sweep.html:78-80) and the row markup separately
     labels the balance cell "real" (line 64) — matches what the code now
     computes for all three columns.
   - New test TestHandleWhatIfConversionSweep_LifetimeTaxIsRealNotNominal
     (handlers_sweep_test.go:351-428): fixture is sweepScenarioSettings(),
     confirmed 30-year horizon (ProjectionYears=30) and 3% inflation
     (models.DefaultWhatIfSettings() sets InflationRate: 3.0, uncontested
     by the fixture) — nonzero multi-year inflation, not a same-value trap.
     It independently re-derives wantReal inline (not by calling
     lifetimeRealTaxAndIRMAA) from a projection it runs itself via
     prepare.From/getEngine().Run, then separately asserts got is close to
     wantReal (tol 0.01) AND asserts got is NOT close to nominal
     BuildTax().TotalTaxPaid. That is not circular — it calls the same
     formula but not the same function, and it independently guards
     (line 394-396) that the fixture's real/nominal actually diverge before
     trusting the rest of the assertions, so a fixture regression to
     inflation=0 would fail loudly rather than silently passing.
     Confirmed passing: `go test -run
     TestHandleWhatIfConversionSweep_LifetimeTaxIsRealNotNominal -v` ->
     PASS. Manifest's claim of reverting the fix and observing the test
     fail (nominal 85409.17 vs real 76482.92) is consistent with the
     magnitude of a 30yr/3% deflation and not independently re-verified by
     me (would require reverting code), but the test's own internal
     nominal-divergence guard (line 394-396) makes a false-positive PASS
     structurally unlikely regardless.

